  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Multiple rounds of showers and thunderstorms \nare expected through Sunday night, with isolated to scattered \ninstances of flash flooding possible.\n\nAn upper level trough will sharpen across the Great Lakes region \nwhile it merges with a closed low over the Ohio Valley tonight \nthrough Sunday night. A cold front extends from the stacked low to a \nweaker area of low pressure in north central Quebec and will slowly \nmove east and become a focus of heavy showers and thunderstorms \nthrough the end of the weekend. \n\nWarm, moist air advection will occur across the region through \ntonight. Cloud cover will increase through this evening with showers \ndeveloping across far western NY late tonight. A deep southerly flow \nwill arrive with a plume of Gulf moisture across the forecast area \nSunday morning. A wavy cold front will settle across far western NY \nto the Saint Lawrence River through Sunday afternoon while a surface \nlow moves across Ohio and into the western Southern Tier. Large \nscale ascent will aid diurnally driven convection within a corridor \nof high moisture content and deep (>11kft) warm cloud depths. \nSoutherly winds will be parallel to the front and result in the \npotential for backbuilding/training as well as repeated rounds of \nstorms.\n\nLow pressure is expected to move along the frontal boundary across \nthe forecast area Sunday night while remaining ahead of the 500mb \ntrough axis. The core of the highest PWATS will shift east \novernight. Heavy showers and thunderstorms are expected across the \nregion with localized areas of heavy rainfall from the Genesee \nValley to the eastern Lake Ontario region. \n\nBased on latest guidance, the axis of heaviest rain is expected \nacross far western NY to the Saint Lawrence River. Basin average \nrainfall amounts will range from 1-2\" near this axis to 0.5-1\" \nfarther east. Localized areas of 2-3\" can't be ruled out. Due to dry \nantecedent conditions across most of the region, opted not to issue \na Flood Watch with this cycle. That being said, isolated to \nscattered instances of flash flooding are possible through Sunday \nnight especially for urban areas and poor drainage areas. \n\nConditions will improve from west to east on Monday. The surface low \nis expected to deepen as a shortwave trough drops south from Canada \nMonday. Showers and thunderstorms with heavy rain are possible \nacross the eastern Lake Ontario region through Monday afternoon. \nHigh pressure builds across the region Monday night through Tuesday \nnight resulting in dry weather. \n\nKey Message 2...Unsettled weather moves back into the region \nWednesday through the end of the week. \n\nThe chance for showers and thunderstorms return to the region \nWednesday through Friday. An upper level trough will move into the \nGreat Lakes region and a return flow will bring moisture northward. \nThe synoptic pattern suggests deep southwesterly flow will persist \nduring this time. While confidence is low regarding timing and \nintensity of showers and thunderstorms, chances are higher in the \nafternoon to evening.",
